  "eng-2007-p1|A suitable title for this passage is|Livestock and Human Casualties of the Bird Flu Pandemic in Nigeria|HIV/AIDS and the Bird Flu Pandemic in Nigeria|The Ravaging Effects of Epidemics in Nigeria|Controlling the Bird Flu Pandemic in Nigeria": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p1|From the passage, it can be inferred that|the Kaduna poultry farmer who lost many birds was not adequately compensated by government|bird flu had already resulted in a high human and livestock death toll|Nigerians no longer consume poultry products as a result of bird flu|bird flu in Nigeria was first discovered by the Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) Diagnostic Centre": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p1|The expression 'to return to the status quo ante' refers to the|state of affairs following the bird flu pandemic in Nigeria|condition of things prior to the incidence of bird flu|bird flu pandemic that had ravaged many countries in Europe, Asia and the Middle East|killing of birds as a result of bird flu and its effects on poultry farmers": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p1|From the third paragraph, the steps taken to check the spread of bird flu in Nigeria were|restricting movement, paying compensation and caging free-range birds|public enlightenment, controlling migratory birds and killing infected birds|monitoring all poultry farms, quarantining affected farms and killing and disposing of affected birds|isolating ravaged farms, destroying and disposing of affected birds and restricting movements": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p1|Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?|The death of many poultry birds led to the discovery of bird flu in Nigeria|If it had not been quickly controlled, bird flu might have proved more deadly than HIV/AIDS|Nigeria's handling of the bird flu pandemic justified African countries' inability to cope with the disaster|Poultry farms in Nigeria had been quarantined": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p2|One conclusion that can be drawn from the opening paragraph is that|lazy people work only to satisfy their own immediate needs|lazy people are thorough-bred loafers who cannot even eat their porridge|though lazy people have ugly faces, they like to see themselves in the mirror|it is a waste of time to advise a lazy man because he will always use a sieve to sift the advice": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p2|From the argument in the last paragraph, it can be concluded that the sluggards are|phlegmatic when advised|weary of sound advice|intolerant of good advice|impervious to advice": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p2|The expression 'if the shower should turn to beer, he might wake himself up a bit' suggests that lazy people|are pleasure-loving|have beer showers|are gourmets|are inconsiderate": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p2|Which of the following explains the point being made in the second paragraph?|The drone and the slothful often look themselves up in the mirror|The drone and the slothful hover around beehives because of their overdependence on honey|The drone and the slothful are idle so they depend on others for their needs|The drone and the sluggards are lazy people who sleep a lot": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p2|Which of the following represents the author's view in the passage?|The slothful will not get out of bed unless someone wakes them up|Idle and unproductive people should have no place in society|Loafers carry their basins about looking for who to fill them with food|Laziness leads to poverty so both should be rejected with equal vehemence": "comprehension",
  "eng-2007-p3|Modern secretaries are different from the old ones because they|are more educated and better exposed to professional training|may become teachers in polytechnics and universities|are able to become graduates of various universities|can use modern gadgets such as computers and other machines": "comprehension",
